   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the vehicle and traffic law, in relation to roundabouts and certain technical corrections relating to lane-use control signal indications, circular intersections and railroad grade crossings",
    "basePrintNo": "S6715",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S6715-2011",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Add §§106 & 140-d, amd §§120, 1116, 1127 & 1685, V & T L",
    "lawSection": "Vehicle and Traffic Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S6715\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn  act  to  amend  the  vehicle  and  traffic  law,  in  relation  to\nroundabouts and certain technical  corrections  relating  to  lane-use\ncontrol  signal indications, circular intersections and railroad grade\ncrossings\n\nPURPOSE OF THE BILL:\n\nThis bill amends certain sections of the Vehicle & Traffic Law (\"VTL\")\nto conform it with  federal  requirements  governing  traffic  control\nfeatures and devices.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection  1  of  the  bill  would create a new VTL § 106 that defines a\n\"circulatory roadway\".\n\nSection 2 of the bill would create a new subdivision (c) to VTL §  120\nthat defines \"circular intersection\".\n\nSection  3  of  the  bill  would create a new VTL § 140-d that defines\n\"roundabout\".\n\nSection 4 of the  bill  would  amend  VTL  §  1116  to  eliminate  the\nreference to a flashing yellow X signal indication and add language to\ninclude a steady white two-way left turn arrow signal indication and a\nsteady white one-way left turn arrow signal indication.\n\nSection  5  of  the  bill  would  amend VTL § 1127 to replace the term\n\"rotary traffic islands\" with \"circular intersections\".\n\nSection 6 of the bill would amend VTL § 1685 to include \"yield\"  signs\nat passive railroad grade crossings.\n\nSection 7 of the bill provides for an immediate effective date.\n\nEXISTING LAW:\n\nThe  VTL  §  1127  refers  to  \"rotary  traffic islands\" and VTL § 120\ndefines \"intersections\".  VTL § 1116(c) allows the use of the flashing\nyellow X as opposed to two different steady white signal  indications.\nVTL  § 1685 allows the use of a \"stop\" sign, but not a \"yield\" sign at\nany highway-rail crossing.\n\nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nThis is a new proposal.\n\nSTATEMENT IN SUPPORT:\n\nStates are required to substantially conform to the National Manual on\nUniform Traffic Control Devices (\"NMUTCD\") or face possible  sanctions\nby the Federal Highway Administration (\"FHWA\"). The current version of\n\n\nthe  NMUTCD  was  adopted  in  January  2010,  and New York's law must\nconform to federal mandates to avoid the possibility of losing federal\nhighway funding. This  bill  brings  New  York  into  compliance  with\nrespect  to  roundabouts,  lane-use  control  signal  indications, and\nhighway-rail grade crossings.\n\nRoundabout and Related Definitions\nCurrently, the VTL refers to \"rotary  traffic  islands,\"  which  is  a\ngeneric  term  for  circular intersections such as traffic circles and\nrotaries.  While  a   \"roundabout\"   looks   similar,   it   functions\ndifferently.  A  roundabout  is a type of a circular intersection with\ncharacteristics not found in other types  of  circular  intersections.\nIn  addition,  a  roundabout  in  New  York State operates as a single\nintersection whereas  the  rotaries  and  circles  are  considered  as\nmultiple  intersections.  Traffic engineers favor roundabouts, because\nthey maintain traffic flow better than  signalized  intersections  but\ngenerally  require  a  smaller  right-of-way  than rotaries or traffic\ncircles.  This  bill  creates  a  definition  for   roundabouts   that\nincorporates   guidance   from  the  FHWA,  which  recommends  that  a\nroundabout  be  specifically  defined  as   a   single   intersection,\nregardless  of  the  size  of  the roundabout.   The addition of these\ndefinitions will ensure that the VTL reflects  the  current  state  of\ntraffic engineering in New York.\n\nLane-Use Control Signal Indications\nLane-use  control signal indications are special overhead signals that\npermit or prohibit the use of specific lanes of a street  or  highway.\nLane-use  control signal indications are distinguished by placement of\nspecial signal faces over a certain lane or lanes of the  roadway  and\nby  their  distinctive  shapes  and  symbols.  Supplementary signs are\nsometimes used to explain their meaning and intent.  Lane-use  control\nsignal indications are most commonly used for reversible-lane control,\nbut  are also used in certain non-reversible lane applications and for\ntoll plaza lanes.\n\nThe NMUTCD requires that a  steady,  white  two-way  left  turn  arrow\nsignal be used to indicate that a lane may be used for left turns, but\nnot  for through travel, with the understanding that common use of the\nlane by oncoming traffic for left turns is also permitted. New  York's\ncurrent  State  Supplement  to the NMUTCD utilizes a flashing yellow X\nsignal in these situations.   The NMUTCD now requires  that  a  steady\nwhite  one-way  left turn arrow signal be used to indicate that a lane\nmay be used for left turns, but not for through  travel,  and  without\nopposing  turns  in  the  same  lane.  New York law does not currently\nmandate any signal for such situations.   The bill  also  makes  minor\nchanges to the provisions for other lane-use control signal indicators\nin  order to conform to the requirements of the NMUTCD. Amending VTL\n1116 will conform New York law with federal law.\n\nHighway-Rail Grade Crossing\nThe NMUTCD mandates that at least one R15-1 crossbuck sign be used  on\neach  highway  approach  to  every  highway-rail  crossing whereas the\nformer 2005 State MUTED allowed  for  the  omission  of  the  railroad\ncrossbuck  where  authorized by the Department of Transportation under\nRailroad Law § 53. Current VTL § 1685 allows the use of a \"stop\"  sign\nbut  not  a  \"yield\" sign at any highway-rail crossing. Amending VTL\n1685 will conform New York law with federal law.\n\n\nBUDGET IMPLICATIONS:\n\nThese  changes  do  not  add  significant  costs  and  compliance will\neliminate the possibility of federal sanctions and the loss of federal\nhighway funds.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n\nThis act would take effect immediately.\n\n",
